+# CasePath cinematic presentation — 24 October 2026
+
+## Requested outcome
+
+The user asks for the live CasePath demo and video to feel deliberately edited, synchronized, simple to understand, and memorable like a television commercial. This is a product-presentation task. No research, inference, live-case writes, external sending, or additional spending is part of it.
+
+## Recovered authority
+
+- Repository: KumarNavish/casepath.
+- Integration branch: codex/casepath-causal-experience-integration-20261009.
+- Exact fetched base: 0ccf1eafcd27e0827e2d0778f9c2642e34cd05f9; existing application release 4864e734.
+- Initial normal clone: casepath-commander-cinema-20261024. Final candidate: casepath-commander-cinema-final-20261024; branch codex/casepath-cinema-final-20261024. The initial candidate and its running gate are preserved.
+- Existing standard 4173 and engineering 4194 instances and their journals are preserved.
+- The parent research context is historical and separate; no scientific writer or research scope is acquired.
+- ChatGPT writes and reasons; Commander executes explicit commands. No Codex CLI, other model runner, or paid API is used.
+
+## Scope and ownership
+
+Root owns visual direction, CSS, presentation helper, final integration, source seal and release decision. The synchronization reviewer owns only startExecutivePresentation and related focused presentation regressions until handoff. The film worker owns capture/export/evidence helpers in this design-plan directory and .runtime/cinema-evidence. Other application, API, corpus, ledger, and source-authority surfaces are out of scope.
+
+## Design decisions
+
+Retain the approved white surfaces, Mulish, restrained crimson, original source cards and process canvas. Fix temporal continuity before adding visual polish. Keep every source, fact, step and result bound to admitted recorded operations. Complete the visible question before moving to the next one. Preserve the exact distinction between a completed check and a positive answer.
+
+Use one coherent opening, restrained in-product orientation, legible reading holds and a deliberate closing payoff. The latest user instruction authorizes cinematic editing and memorable presentation; concise editorial framing and an optional original soundtrack deliberately extend the older continuous-take/silent-only brief. They may explain recorded behavior but cannot fabricate operational progress, legal conclusions, qualified review, or saved checklist creation. No cosmetic motion may imply that a real workflow is executing.
+
+The film must start at the admitted presentation, not include cold network preparation. The live page must still show honest preparation and failure states. Any soundtrack is original local synthesis, optional, and bound to the same manifest; the silent product remains intelligible.
+
+## Required completion evidence
+
+1. Fresh before/after frame inspection and a preserved baseline.
+2. Focused regressions for question order, source bindings, transition continuity, stable scene identity, pause/seek and reduced motion.
+3. A complete browser walkthrough with zero unexpected requests, writes, or console errors; relevant desktop/mobile geometry checks.
+4. Sealed source and the repository's full release gate before publishing a candidate.
+5. Fresh film, media metadata, sampled frames and an independent cold-viewer review; repair any substantive miss.
+6. Matching deployed frontend/API source identities if released, preserving public read-only audience and existing usage/data.
+7. Exact commit, deliverable links and an honest receipt distinguishing observed results from limits.
+
+This brief records scope and intended verification; it is not completion evidence.
+
+## Implemented editorial review — 24 October 2026
+
+The film keeps the approved white/Mulish/crimson product language. It adds chapter orientation, a larger opening, a restrained progress line and a full closing composition. The narrative is customer question → ordered source review and accepted process → reviewable assessment and unsent draft → checklist reuse with fresh evidence → closing promise.
+
+The preserved operation identity and the accepted-history prefix are separate when an earlier extraction is revisited. The film explicitly labels that moment “Reviewing recorded evidence”. A node never appears to become unchecked after being accepted. “Condensed replay” replaces the old numerical 4× claim because the actual edit compresses motion and holds differently.
+
+Each scene transition owns entry opacity once. Matching scene structures preserve their DOM and scroll positions across cursor, highlight and hold phases. Direct and backward seeks have the same visible state. The later case remains visible during its establishing hold.
+
+A proposed negative fact now says “Proposed: not supported”. Typography adds empty layout spans to visibly separate concatenated currency/date tokens and statutory paragraphs; textContent and Unicode citation offsets remain exact. The later-email label describes this case’s chronology instead of asserting a general legal rule. The zero-missing-file label is explicitly limited to checklist scope.
+
+### Current checks and independent review
+
+- Official focused presentation browser test passed, with 5,913 frame samples, all six acts, exact source bindings, no unintended content overlaps, monotonic accepted nodes, stable DOM, deterministic seeking, and transition continuity.
+- The actual 24-frame preseal capture passed with no browser errors, application writes or unexpected destinations. Logical viewport 1600×900 at DPR 1.2 produces 1920×1080 output. The edited clock is 98.55 seconds.
+- A fresh independent assistant reviewed 24 evenly spaced frames plus detailed source, law, answer and reuse scenes. Its initial readability and overly broad label findings were fixed and rechecked. Its final verdict had no remaining blocking issue in the static review.
+- Its remembered takeaway: CasePath makes a legal case assessment traceable to its evidence and lets the same checks be reused on a new case.
+- Static visual review does not establish animation quality or qualified domain approval. Clock/geometry tests establish the implemented timing and layout invariants. No qualified human or legal approval is claimed.
+- Full exact-commit gate, deployment and public capture are subsequent release steps, with their own actual receipts.
+
+### Deliberate differences from the concepts
+
+Matched: palette, Mulish, role navigation, source rail, actual process questions, evidence connector, completed process, three-card reuse structure and human review boundary.
+
+Deliberate: three recorded rent-increase steps instead of illustrative concept content; exact actual passages and checklist provenance; chapter titles, opening/closing typography and a progress line for the current cinematic brief; longer assessment reading hold; conditional proposed-fact verdict; honest checklist reuse instead of implying expert review or newly learned memory.
+
+The current request authorizes cinematic editing of the demo and movie. The product clock still supplies every visible state; the export does not manufacture operations, sources, results, completed work, sending, or new provider calls.
+
+## Final release candidate follow-up
+
+The first full gate at 619c2540f0df56a9f96dd517e0d0d736b5a99570 surfaced an obsolete frontend assertion expecting “All files received”. The intentional product wording is “Checklist scope”. The final candidate updates that exact assertion, including the closing small tag, while retaining the zero-missing count, process-verdict, source-title, citation and immutable-state checks. Product JavaScript, CSS and index bytes remain identical to the first cinematic candidate. This candidate still requires its own source seal and fresh full gate; no success is inferred from the earlier run.
